# Scroll-State Interpretation

Read the original instructions.md unchanged. This supplement clarifies the
existing cross-native-screen coverage contract; it does not waive lost content
or provide visual approval.

The renderer already supports overflow-x:auto in exercise bodies and choice
option bodies. Native screenshots deliberately retain different horizontal
scroll positions so every glyph and formula-box region can be observed.
contexts/batchN.record provides the actual saved scroll state, not new pixels.

A formula's partial prefix or suffix at a known reachable horizontal scroll
frame is not alone proof of inaccessible internal clipping. Record the visible
glyphs, the partial edge and the actual scroll state. Judge their legibility and
spacing from pixels. Do not claim the whole formula or option is visually
complete until its complementary native screenshots have actually been observed.
The final gate joins those observations. A hidden/clip container, unreachable
content, overlap, lost mathematical mark or unresolved coverage still blocks it.

Never open a screenshot outside your assigned batch, including earlier PASS
images. Do not open the entire runtime report just to inspect its large target
lists. Every assigned image is opened once, no crop/resize/reopen/rerender.
Keep the original result schema, truthful accounting and unknown costs.
This supplement does not authorize changing app, content, dependencies or skills.
